WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.544
- How many owners have really come
out and spoke on our behalf?

00:00:02.877 --> 00:00:05.630
How many of these college coaches have
come out and spoke on our behalf that

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:09.259
ride these Black players in football and
basketball to where they're the highest-paid

00:00:09.259 --> 00:00:13.680
college coaches around the country and their
programs are benefiting? Not too many.

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:16.433
And to me, their silence speaks volumes.

00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:17.809
- I'm Taylor Rooks.

00:00:17.809 --> 00:00:22.856
And these are conversations about
defining one's relationship to Blackness

00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:25.108
and how that defines their America.

00:00:28.903 --> 00:00:33.658
So, when did you realize that being
Black was different than being white?

00:00:34.993 --> 00:00:39.748
- I learned early. Being
biracial, born in San Jose,

00:00:39.831 --> 00:00:46.004
I was, you know, as early as I can remember,
I was amongst Black and Mexican kids.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:51.968
But I moved to Sacramento
at nine, eight or nine, and

00:00:51.968 --> 00:00:53.636
I went from predominantly

00:00:54.095 --> 00:00:56.389
Mexican and Black to all-white schools.

00:00:57.182 --> 00:01:03.021
So in third grade, being new at
this school, I found out very early

00:01:03.021 --> 00:01:06.775
on that I was Black, and it was because
the kids wouldn't let me play anything.

00:01:06.983 --> 00:01:09.527
Couldn't play basketball with them,
wouldn’t let me play kickball with them,

00:01:09.527 --> 00:01:10.862
wouldn’t let me throw the football with them.

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:17.911
And instead of, you know, poor me
hugging, consoled when I went home,

00:01:18.369 --> 00:01:20.663
you know, my dad said, “If
they call you nigger, fight ‘em.”

00:01:21.122 --> 00:01:26.336
So I learned in third grade that,
you know, if kids were—I didn't know

00:01:26.336 --> 00:01:29.422
it was racial or racism at the time—but
if they called me nigger, cuz that's what

00:01:29.422 --> 00:01:31.341
the kids were calling me, to fight ‘em.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:36.304
So I learned fairly
early that my skin color

00:01:36.888 --> 00:01:39.349
made some people just not
like me without even knowing me.

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:42.769
- How many fights did you get in?

00:01:43.144 --> 00:01:46.022
- Oh, I got in so many fights that my
mom had to come work at my school.

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:51.361
And my mom came and worked as,
like, a yard duty on her off time at

00:01:51.361 --> 00:01:53.071
my schools because it was constant.

00:01:53.321 --> 00:01:57.534
And I would tell the school, and
nothing would really happen,

00:01:57.826 --> 00:02:01.704
you know? So it got to a point where
my mom was just on campus during

00:02:01.913 --> 00:02:06.126
the recess and during lunchtime,
just to kind of somewhat keep me out

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:09.587
of trouble but somewhat keep kids
away from me that were, you know, harassing me.

00:02:11.172 --> 00:02:14.342
- In what ways do you feel like you
yourself were treated differently?

00:02:14.342 --> 00:02:19.055
Maybe not by kids, but just by the
white people in the town that you lived.

00:02:19.722 --> 00:02:20.390
- Um...

00:02:21.266 --> 00:02:24.936
that I was just different, you know? Like
I said, my neighborhood was multicultural.

00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:30.608
We had everything in my neighborhood,
but my parents early on made a point

00:02:30.608 --> 00:02:33.862
to put me in better schools, and better
schools where I was from meant

00:02:33.862 --> 00:02:38.408
white schools, you know? So I never
understood why. You know, I was so, like,

00:02:38.408 --> 00:02:41.369
“Why can't I go to school with my friends in the
neighborhood?” and all that kind of stuff,

00:02:42.412 --> 00:02:49.377
and I was never allowed to. And I guess
it set me up long-term to just kind of be

00:02:49.377 --> 00:02:51.296
more versatile and more of a chameleon.

00:02:51.296 --> 00:02:56.176
And even though, like I said, I'm
half-Italian, my first eight, nine years,

00:02:56.176 --> 00:02:58.094
I was never really around
white kids, you know?

00:02:58.094 --> 00:02:59.429
So to be thrust into that
position in

00:02:59.429 --> 00:02:59.554
So to be thrust into that
position in
third grade

00:02:59.554 --> 00:03:00.972
third grade

00:03:02.557 --> 00:03:03.808
was a culture shock for me,

00:03:04.184 --> 00:03:07.270
one, and then two, from the
educational side, I went from

00:03:07.896 --> 00:03:12.692
being—going into third grade in San Jose,
moving and then being in third grade in

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:15.987
Sacramento. I got—and I realized what
the educational system was like too,

00:03:15.987 --> 00:03:17.739
cuz I went from third grade there to

00:03:18.281 --> 00:03:20.867
they had some of my classes
back in the kindergarten class.

00:03:20.992 --> 00:03:28.124
So I'm this big old Billy Madison-type kid, bigger.
- [Laughing] Yeah.
- Because the schooling

00:03:28.124 --> 00:03:31.711
where I was at was obviously below
par, you know? So I learned very

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:34.422
quickly at about age nine that

00:03:35.298 --> 00:03:39.594
color and race played a huge factor in my life.

00:03:40.678 --> 00:03:43.473
- You know, you've mentioned, you
know, being biracial, being half-Italian,

00:03:43.473 --> 00:03:47.685
and there's obviously—you
know, Black people are not a monolith.

00:03:47.769 --> 00:03:49.604
Everybody's experience is different,

00:03:49.604 --> 00:03:51.522
just like everybody's DNA is different.
- Right.

00:03:51.522 --> 00:03:55.944
- Not everybody's Blackness is the same.
Being half-white and being half-Black,

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:59.447
do you feel like you had to choose?

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:02.158
- I never chose.

00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:07.080
And to be honest with you, I never
really fit in because as a biracial

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.290
child, you're never white enough
and you're never Black enough.

00:04:09.666 --> 00:04:10.250
You know what I mean?

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:14.003
So I wasn't dark enough,
and I had this “good hair.”

00:04:14.337 --> 00:04:18.633
And then with the white kids,
I was darker than them and

00:04:19.217 --> 00:04:22.762
just a different complexion and different
mannerisms, you know? So I was never,

00:04:23.429 --> 00:04:25.014
I never really had anywhere to run.

00:04:25.014 --> 00:04:27.308
You know, now being biracial
is more of the norm.

00:04:27.308 --> 00:04:29.644
And I think in the next 10
years, 15 years, everyone's

00:04:29.644 --> 00:04:31.646
gonna be mixed up somewhat.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.106
But I was really,

00:04:33.398 --> 00:04:36.818
I was by myself. I was on an
island until I really got a chance.

00:04:36.818 --> 00:04:39.946
And I think through sports, once
these kids, once they were finally

00:04:39.946 --> 00:04:42.740
tired of getting beat up, they
finally allowed me to play with them.

00:04:42.949 --> 00:04:45.410
And then once they allowed me to
play with them and I was the best at,

00:04:45.868 --> 00:04:49.831
you know, football, basketball,
baseball, kickball. Through sports

00:04:49.831 --> 00:04:53.293
is what allowed them to get the
chance to get to know me as a person.

00:04:53.751 --> 00:04:59.549
So that was kind of the turning point.
And oddly enough, I still have friends from

00:04:59.549 --> 00:05:03.553
third grade that, not necessarily that
I got into fights with, but that were

00:05:03.553 --> 00:05:07.181
actually some of the first kids to kinda
accept me and allow me to kinda show who

00:05:07.181 --> 00:05:11.894
I was through sports. And, man, almost 30
years later, you know, I'm still friends with them.

00:05:13.062 --> 00:05:17.108
- Now, of course, you know, being Black
and white, you have lighter skin.

00:05:17.734 --> 00:05:21.446
So I kinda want to talk to you about
colorism, not just in the Black community,

00:05:21.446 --> 00:05:27.076
but how white people respond to colorism
or lighter-skinned Black people.

00:05:27.785 --> 00:05:31.247
How do you think colorism
has maybe affected your life?

00:05:31.247 --> 00:05:34.459
And have you seen people
respond to you differently

00:05:34.584 --> 00:05:37.253
because you maybe have
more European features,

00:05:37.253 --> 00:05:38.171
you have lighter skin?

00:05:38.796 --> 00:05:39.922
- I don't think

00:05:40.256 --> 00:05:41.841
colorism has affected me

00:05:42.342 --> 00:05:49.349
as much as racism has. Racism has been
heavier, although colorism does exist.

00:05:49.557 --> 00:05:52.643
And definitely, I feel like in our
Black community—and when I say

00:05:53.227 --> 00:05:56.522
Black, I feel like these days, if
you have a drop of Black in you,

00:05:56.522 --> 00:05:58.024
you're Black, you know what I mean?

00:05:58.024 --> 00:06:04.864
So I learned at 17—um, 18, excuse
me—right before I was about to graduate

00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:08.576
high school and go to UCLA in 1998, there
was a situation in my high school.

00:06:08.576 --> 00:06:14.707
My sister was a sophomore, and a boy
was calling her nigger, spit in her hair,

00:06:15.083 --> 00:06:16.376
and I never ran into this kid.

00:06:16.376 --> 00:06:20.171
And finally, one day she came
to me as my fourth period ended.

00:06:20.171 --> 00:06:22.632
I was going home for the day
and told me, “This boy called me

00:06:22.632 --> 00:06:23.925
a nigger again and spit in my hair.”

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:27.261
So I went and happened to
just see the kid walking by.

00:06:27.261 --> 00:06:30.431
So I did what a big brother does, you
know? Beat him up, tried to go to the

00:06:30.431 --> 00:06:31.599
office and explain what happened,

00:06:31.599 --> 00:06:32.350
and they didn't believe me.

00:06:32.392 --> 00:06:34.602
So keeping this in mind, I'm
the No. 1 football player,

00:06:34.977 --> 00:06:36.229
No. 1 basketball player.

00:06:36.229 --> 00:06:38.022
Everyone knows I'm going to UCLA.

00:06:38.689 --> 00:06:42.485
But here I'm in the midst of, you
know, kind of a racial situation where

00:06:42.485 --> 00:06:44.445
my school doesn't believe me.

00:06:44.487 --> 00:06:46.572
So to this day, I don't give a
dollar back to my high school

00:06:46.572 --> 00:06:47.990
because of this particular incident.

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:51.536
So anyway, I get suspended for a
week, and while I'm suspended, my

00:06:51.536 --> 00:06:53.329
school was vandalized by the KKK.

00:06:53.663 --> 00:06:58.418
So, burnt down a bathroom, vandalized,
broken windows, swastikas everywhere,

00:06:58.459 --> 00:07:01.629
“Die, nigger.” They had a mannequin hanging
from the big tree in front of my

00:07:01.629 --> 00:07:04.298
school with my football jersey on
that said “Die, nigger.”

00:07:06.384 --> 00:07:11.264
So, I realized at 17 or 18 that
even though I was very proud to be

00:07:11.431 --> 00:07:14.851
biracial and Italian and Black, that
the world looked at me as a Black man.

00:07:15.685 --> 00:07:18.688
So it wasn't, to answer your
other question, I never really

00:07:18.688 --> 00:07:20.815
chose. The world chose for me.

00:07:22.442 --> 00:07:26.487
- You said something I think is important,
which is, you know, “I was the No. 1

00:07:26.487 --> 00:07:29.782
basketball player, No. 1
football player, but it didn't matter.”

00:07:30.491 --> 00:07:34.370
Because I think a lot of times
when we are, you know, in the

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:38.958
heat of these racial movements,
people will say, “What do you know?

00:07:38.958 --> 00:07:42.503
You're this, you're that. America has
afforded you these great things.

00:07:42.503 --> 00:07:44.630
You don't even know what
it means to be, right,

00:07:44.672 --> 00:07:48.009
like, to have, to be affected by racism”[a][b]
because that's not what your life

00:07:48.009 --> 00:07:52.221
reflects. But they're not understanding
that whether you're a football player,

00:07:52.221 --> 00:07:55.099
basketball player, famous person, you're
still Black at the end of the day.
- Absolutely.

00:07:56.142 --> 00:08:00.188
- And I don't know if that is a
thing that registers. Like, racism

00:08:00.188 --> 00:08:01.814
doesn't have to do with your status.

00:08:01.814 --> 00:08:05.026
It's simply has to do
with the color of your skin.

00:08:05.276 --> 00:08:08.029
- You know, having younger children,
I talked to them about that. And

00:08:08.029 --> 00:08:13.868
my twins are half-Black, a
quarter-Italian and a quarter-Mexican,

00:08:14.076 --> 00:08:15.161
you know, so they're very

00:08:15.453 --> 00:08:15.828
mixed.

00:08:16.287 --> 00:08:20.500
And I'm thankful from a standpoint of
everything I faced growing up that

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:22.251
you can't really tell what my kids are.

00:08:22.293 --> 00:08:28.049
And like I said, in 2020, it's more of,
you know, biracial is around a

00:08:28.049 --> 00:08:31.886
lot more than it was in the early ‘80s
when I was coming up as a child, because

00:08:31.886 --> 00:08:36.057
it was, you know, you were Mexican, [c][d]you were Black,
you were white, you were Asian. There wasn't really

00:08:36.057 --> 00:08:38.434
too much mixing going on at that time.

00:08:38.434 --> 00:08:41.521
So, you know, I have conversations
with my sons cuz they're old

00:08:41.521 --> 00:08:42.772
enough to understand stuff right now.

00:08:42.772 --> 00:08:44.440
We talked about the George Floyd stuff

00:08:45.399 --> 00:08:46.150
and simple stuff.

00:08:46.359 --> 00:08:50.071
And, you know, one of my sons asked
me, like, “So, you're trying to tell me

00:08:50.071 --> 00:08:52.532
that someone's gonna hate me just
cuz the color of my skin?”

00:08:52.949 --> 00:08:56.702
And I said, “I hope not, but it's
definitely a possibility,” you know? So

00:08:56.702 --> 00:08:59.956
for children to kind of grasp that, you
know? My kids aren't gonna grow up

00:08:59.956 --> 00:09:04.085
like I grew up: food stamps, drug-filled
environment, abuse. Obviously, you know,

00:09:04.085 --> 00:09:07.380
I've worked my butt off to put them
in private schools, and we live behind

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:12.009
gates. Myself and their mom live behind
gates, so they haven't experienced it.

00:09:12.301 --> 00:09:14.929
And I hope they don't,
but I'm sure at one point

00:09:15.137 --> 00:09:16.389
they will.

00:09:16.764 --> 00:09:18.349
So I just kinda want
them to be prepared.

00:09:18.474 --> 00:09:18.933
You know what I mean?

00:09:18.933 --> 00:09:21.477
I think we have to start educating
our kids at an early age, knowing

00:09:21.477 --> 00:09:23.020
that this is a, this is a cold world.

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:26.399
Although I feel now more than
ever, we're at a turning point,

00:09:26.774 --> 00:09:30.152
with this stuff and
things are starting to get done.

00:09:30.611 --> 00:09:33.489
I still really feel that that hate
will never go anywhere because it's

00:09:33.489 --> 00:09:36.909
a taught trait, and there's still too
many people in this world that hate

00:09:37.034 --> 00:09:38.536
and teach their children to hate.

00:09:38.536 --> 00:09:41.163
So, you know, children
turn into adults that hate.

00:09:41.163 --> 00:09:41.539
So,

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:48.588
I'm very open with my kids
when it comes to situations that they

00:09:48.588 --> 00:09:50.673
need to be aware of, and race is

00:09:50.673 --> 00:09:51.632
definitely one of them.

00:09:51.632 --> 00:09:56.721
- When you're talking to your kids, how often do

00:09:56.721 --> 00:09:59.849
you stress, or do you stress, that

00:10:00.891 --> 00:10:05.521
for them to maybe succeed or be accepted,

00:10:05.563 --> 00:10:07.023
they have to be excellent?

00:10:07.064 --> 00:10:11.193
And the reason I ask that is because
I went into a talk where Lena Waithe was

00:10:11.193 --> 00:10:12.695
discussing her movie Queen &amp; Slim,

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:16.449
and there is a line in the
movie where he's talking about

00:10:16.449 --> 00:10:17.867
how he wants to be the best.

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:21.037
And she says, “Why do Black people
always have to be excellent?”

00:10:21.996 --> 00:10:25.458
And I think a lot of that is
because, like she said, we think

00:10:25.458 --> 00:10:29.879
we have to be excellent in order to
justify the space that we take up.

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:34.592
Whereas, you know, I don't think white
people, the majority of white people, say

00:10:34.592 --> 00:10:36.969
like, you know, “I have to be the best doctor.”

00:10:37.261 --> 00:10:41.515
They can just be a doctor, you know?
Or “I have to be the best teacher.”

00:10:41.515 --> 00:10:45.770
They can just—they are afforded the
luxury of not having to be exceptional

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:51.108
at it because they don't have to explain
to people why they are where they are.

00:10:51.359 --> 00:10:53.903
- Well, I just think the reason why that
happens is because, you know, if we're

00:10:53.903 --> 00:10:56.947
lucky enough to get into these fields
or these professions, we don't get

00:10:57.198 --> 00:10:58.866
a chance to make mistakes, so to speak.

00:10:59.033 --> 00:10:59.575
You know what I mean?

00:10:59.575 --> 00:11:03.204
So a person, a white person, can be
very comfortable being a doctor,

00:11:03.204 --> 00:11:04.330
lawyer, whatever the situation,

00:11:05.289 --> 00:11:07.667
and if you mess up, you're most
likely gonna get another chance.

00:11:07.667 --> 00:11:11.921
And I compare this really in
sports and with Black head coaches

00:11:12.338 --> 00:11:15.383
having to really be careful picking
their jobs because most of the time

00:11:15.383 --> 00:11:18.010
where there's a coaching
change, it’s because it's a bad team.

00:11:18.552 --> 00:11:23.474
So a white coach can go in there and be
subpar and get fired and get another job.

00:11:23.557 --> 00:11:27.353
But a Black coach, you know,
you don't get too many chances.

00:11:27.645 --> 00:11:30.439
- I think white people historically
have been afforded the

00:11:30.439 --> 00:11:33.025
opportunity to fail up.
- Right.

00:11:33.067 --> 00:11:36.278
- And Black people just
do not get to fail up.

00:11:36.278 --> 00:11:37.738
They only get to fail.

00:11:38.114 --> 00:11:41.701
And more than that, it's like
when one Black person fails, it's

00:11:41.701 --> 00:11:43.202
like all the Black people failed.

00:11:44.995 --> 00:11:45.329
- Right.
- Yeah.

00:11:45.329 --> 00:11:48.666
It's all completely,
completely grouped together.

00:11:48.666 --> 00:11:52.253
But when you talk about, you know,
how certain, like, white coaches have

00:11:52.253 --> 00:11:54.922
been able to bounce around, even if
they're losing, they get new jobs.

00:11:55.172 --> 00:11:58.884
When we talk about diversity in
sports or diversity in any workplace,

00:11:59.218 --> 00:12:02.638
it isn't simply so that you can
have these numbers to check off

00:12:02.638 --> 00:12:03.889
that you have a person of color.

00:12:04.557 --> 00:12:09.186
It actually really does improve the
place that you work, because maybe the

00:12:09.186 --> 00:12:12.314
reason none of these teams are getting
better is because you're continuing

00:12:12.314 --> 00:12:16.861
to hire the same people, and hiring
different people would improve it.

00:12:17.153 --> 00:12:17.278
- You know,

00:12:17.278 --> 00:12:19.113
so that's— know we can cross over.

00:12:19.113 --> 00:12:22.491
We're just not getting the
opportunity to cross over.

00:12:22.491 --> 00:12:22.783
-Yeah.
- But let me ask you—

00:12:23.242 --> 00:12:29.373
- Yeah, go ahead.
- What is it like for for young, Black, coming up in a space

00:12:29.373 --> 00:12:31.542
like this that has predominantly been

00:12:31.959 --> 00:12:34.253
white women in your
position, or white men in your

00:12:34.253 --> 00:12:37.840
position that are doing these, this
kind of stuff that you're doing?

00:12:38.382 --> 00:12:38.674
- Yeah.

00:12:38.674 --> 00:12:44.805
I mean, you know, I always say I
take a lot of pride in the fact that

00:12:45.389 --> 00:12:48.184
I am a Black woman who looks Black.

00:12:48.225 --> 00:12:52.605
And I say that because growing up,
when I was looking at any woman on

00:12:52.605 --> 00:12:57.860
TV specifically covering sports,
they were white, blonde hair, and

00:12:57.860 --> 00:12:59.028
if they weren't white or blonde

00:12:59.028 --> 00:12:59.820
and they were Black,

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:00.488
they had

00:13:00.863 --> 00:13:03.908
long hair, little noses, very light skin.

00:13:04.408 --> 00:13:10.164
It was—everybody on TV was almost
tailored to make white people comfortable.

00:13:10.831 --> 00:13:16.462
And I take a lot of pride and
responsibility in being in this space and

00:13:16.462 --> 00:13:20.341
talking a lot about Black people, both
for Black people and to Black people.

00:13:20.341 --> 00:13:21.425
Cuz I think it's important,

00:13:21.425 --> 00:13:25.179
and I wish it's something that I
always had when I was growing up.

00:13:25.554 --> 00:13:29.391
But my experience is always going
to be different than any man's, Black or white.
- Period.

00:13:29.391 --> 00:13:33.312
- And definitely
different than any white woman.

00:13:33.771 --> 00:13:35.606
But I find a lot of strength in that.

00:13:35.648 --> 00:13:37.483
You know, I tell people all the time,

00:13:37.483 --> 00:13:41.070
I think one of the biggest advantages
that I have as an interviewer is

00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:42.863
that I'm a woman and that I am Black.

00:13:43.531 --> 00:13:47.493
I think athletes just inherently
feel more comfortable talking

00:13:47.493 --> 00:13:48.828
to someone who looks like them.

00:13:49.286 --> 00:13:50.496
- Do you find that—

00:13:50.538 --> 00:13:54.583
well, like you said, you found it to
be a strength, but can it also be a

00:13:54.583 --> 00:13:57.378
negative point of view because one,
you're a woman in sports, and then

00:13:57.378 --> 00:13:59.380
two, you're a Black woman in sports?

00:13:59.421 --> 00:14:03.968
So although you've turned it into a
strength, was it ever a hindrance in

00:14:03.968 --> 00:14:07.388
your process, or do you know any of your
colleagues who were in the same position

00:14:07.388 --> 00:14:10.850
where it's held them back for those two
reasons that you’ve turned into strengths?

00:14:11.225 --> 00:14:12.184
- Yeah, for sure.

00:14:12.184 --> 00:14:15.479
I mean, you know, I'm very close with
Cari [Champion], I'm friends with Ros [Gold-Onwude], and

00:14:15.479 --> 00:14:17.731
we talk about these
things all of the time.

00:14:18.357 --> 00:14:22.778
I would say the hindrance hasn't
necessarily come from being Black as

00:14:22.778 --> 00:14:27.032
much as it has come from the perception
of Black people or Black women.

00:14:27.783 --> 00:14:28.075
I think

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:31.537
people oversexualize Black women.

00:14:31.954 --> 00:14:32.162
- Mm.

00:14:32.788 --> 00:14:36.876
- And they have, historically. It
doesn't matter what we're doing,

00:14:37.293 --> 00:14:38.919
we become oversexualized.

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:46.802
And we are the only people that will
be talking to a person or doing an interview,

00:14:46.802 --> 00:14:49.763
and somebody says, “Ooh, like,
they’d be cute together”

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:55.019
or “Ooh, are they dating?” simply because
we’re two Black people and a Black

00:14:55.019 --> 00:14:57.062
woman. It doesn't matter what I wear.

00:14:57.354 --> 00:15:01.025
I could be wearing a
turtleneck and slacks,

00:15:02.026 --> 00:15:04.445
and the conversation is
about what I look like.

00:15:04.445 --> 00:15:07.865
And that is because we
oversexualize Black women.

00:15:08.407 --> 00:15:14.455
And I mean that about white men and
Black men. That is something that I think

00:15:14.455 --> 00:15:16.749
Black men have to be a bit better at

00:15:17.499 --> 00:15:23.130
is seeing Black women as whole
women and not just seeing them

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:24.840
and their relationship to them,

00:15:25.215 --> 00:15:26.258
if that makes sense.

00:15:27.259 --> 00:15:30.596
- I never thought about it from that point
of view. I've never looked at it and made

00:15:30.596 --> 00:15:33.599
those assumptions. But, you know, being
friends with Cari, you know, these are

00:15:33.599 --> 00:15:35.059
some of the conversations we have too.

00:15:35.059 --> 00:15:38.604
And she tells me, you know, that “I'm
wearing a simple dress with my legs out,

00:15:38.604 --> 00:15:42.191
and all of a sudden, I'm too sexy. But, you know,
my counterparts are wearing the same thing,

00:15:42.191 --> 00:15:44.735
and just cuz she doesn't have
as much sun on her body,

00:15:44.735 --> 00:15:45.819
it's not a problem.”

00:15:46.195 --> 00:15:46.737
- Yeah.

00:15:46.737 --> 00:15:46.987
Yeah.

00:15:47.404 --> 00:15:47.988
Absolutely.

00:15:48.948 --> 00:15:50.407
And I also think

00:15:51.367 --> 00:15:56.413
it's so—that conversation is so
layered—but I think Black women are

00:15:56.413 --> 00:15:59.708
left out of a lot of conversations.

00:15:59.792 --> 00:16:05.047
And I think Breonna Taylor is a perfect
example of that, that she has been

00:16:05.047 --> 00:16:07.091
lost in a lot of ways in this movement.

00:16:07.216 --> 00:16:10.761
And I'm glad that now, you know,
everybody is calling for her murderers to

00:16:10.761 --> 00:16:14.848
be arrested, and then you now have the
Breonna Law that was passed because of her, but—

00:16:17.142 --> 00:16:18.018
- How do you have—me, what pisses me off,

00:16:18.018 --> 00:16:19.603
not to cut you off, but
how do you have a law

00:16:20.354 --> 00:16:21.605
for her now, but haven't

00:16:21.605 --> 00:16:24.233
arrested the cops?
- That makes no sense.

00:16:24.233 --> 00:16:26.527
- Those don't go together.

00:16:26.527 --> 00:16:29.571
- Yeah, you're admitting there was some
type of wrongdoing.

00:16:29.905 --> 00:16:30.906
- But you haven't arrested them.

00:16:30.906 --> 00:16:32.950
- However, there's no consequence to the wrongdoers.

00:16:32.950 --> 00:16:34.368
And I just think that's crazy.

00:16:34.827 --> 00:16:37.788
- especially after seeing the police report,
which is insane, but go ahead.

00:16:38.163 --> 00:16:38.747
- Yeah, no.

00:16:38.747 --> 00:16:38.914
Yeah.

00:16:38.914 --> 00:16:44.044
It's the Breonna Taylor thing is
just so sad and, you know, any

00:16:44.044 --> 00:16:49.008
time I'm on my phone and I see, you
know, Black men dying, it hurts, but

00:16:49.008 --> 00:16:54.638
to see someone who absolutely could
be you...like, Breonna Taylor is me.

00:16:54.638 --> 00:16:55.472
She's my friend.

00:16:55.472 --> 00:16:57.349
She is a Black woman who was working

00:16:57.349 --> 00:16:58.058
and was just

00:16:58.726 --> 00:16:59.727
in her apartment.
- Right.

00:16:59.977 --> 00:17:04.231
- And I think that that hit a lot of us
differently, and I think Black women just

00:17:04.231 --> 00:17:10.112
wanna continue to be supported the
way that Black women support Black men.

00:17:11.196 --> 00:17:11.613
- I hear you.

00:17:11.905 --> 00:17:12.322
I hear you.

00:17:13.407 --> 00:17:15.409
- Cuz, like, nobody goes harder for Black men

00:17:15.909 --> 00:17:17.119
than Black women.

00:17:17.494 --> 00:17:22.124
- If you ever listen to Tupac’s song called "White
Man'z World" off the Makaveli album,

00:17:22.124 --> 00:17:25.586
it says that, you know, Black
women are an endangered species.

00:17:25.586 --> 00:17:27.713
So you guys have the
toughest job in this world,

00:17:27.838 --> 00:17:28.422
you know what I mean?

00:17:28.422 --> 00:17:32.468
To just, just exist, let
alone elevate yourself,

00:17:32.676 --> 00:17:34.511
you know? Like I said, cuz
you're behind the eight ball twice.
- Yeah.

00:17:35.179 --> 00:17:37.931
- You know, you're a woman, you're
Black, and don't be a lesbian.

00:17:38.390 --> 00:17:38.932
You know what I mean?

00:17:38.932 --> 00:17:41.727
So then all three things are
really stacked against you.

00:17:41.727 --> 00:17:46.899
So I take pride in seeing women like
you and Cari and Lena and Ava [DuVernay] and

00:17:46.899 --> 00:17:51.612
all these women, you know, exceed
expectations because although they're

00:17:51.612 --> 00:17:55.783
killing us and doing all this stuff,
I still feel it's a lot harder for

00:17:55.783 --> 00:17:57.868
Black women to climb the ladder,

00:17:58.160 --> 00:18:01.205
so to speak, than it is
for Black men.

00:18:01.872 --> 00:18:02.331
- Yeah.

00:18:02.372 --> 00:18:03.665
No, I totally agree.

00:18:03.999 --> 00:18:07.336
You touched on something in there
though that I wanna get your

00:18:07.336 --> 00:18:13.092
opinion on. What do you think, or
how would you describe, is the Black

00:18:13.092 --> 00:18:15.135
athlete’s relationship with the media?

00:18:16.095 --> 00:18:16.804
- Um...

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:21.809
- Seeing it from you as an athlete and
now you as a media member that—

00:18:22.476 --> 00:18:24.103
- I don’t want, because it's—I don't want it—

00:18:24.436 --> 00:18:25.604
I'm not even gonna make that analogy

00:18:25.604 --> 00:18:26.688
cuz it's not the right time.

00:18:26.939 --> 00:18:27.898
They just don't trust ‘em.

00:18:28.649 --> 00:18:29.191
You know what I mean?

00:18:29.191 --> 00:18:32.194
And I think that's the reason why
myself and Stephen Jackson have been

00:18:32.194 --> 00:18:37.658
able to succeed not only on ESPN and
Fox, but on our podcast, All the Smoke,

00:18:37.658 --> 00:18:40.744
because there's no hidden agendas.
We're not looking for clickbait.

00:18:40.953 --> 00:18:43.539
We genuinely wanna have
a conversation with you and

00:18:43.539 --> 00:18:45.040
humanize you and show the world

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:45.707
the other side of you.

00:18:46.208 --> 00:18:47.584
- You mentioned Jack,

00:18:47.584 --> 00:18:50.879
and I just wanna give you a chance
to talk about maybe how you have

00:18:50.879 --> 00:18:54.800
been moved by him during this time,
inspired by him during this time.

00:18:54.800 --> 00:19:00.722
Cuz I think for so many of us,
it's just, it has been something in

00:19:00.722 --> 00:19:05.060
this movement that has been beautiful,
and I think we've needed to hold on

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:06.895
to some things that are beautiful.
- Yes.

00:19:07.521 --> 00:19:10.566
- And he's just, he's had
some really beautiful moments

00:19:10.566 --> 00:19:11.859
that inspired us all.

00:19:12.359 --> 00:19:15.028
- You know, just as a brother
of mine, I couldn't

00:19:15.028 --> 00:19:16.738
have been more proud of him.

00:19:16.989 --> 00:19:18.282
You know, Jack has kind of—

00:19:20.284 --> 00:19:24.830
there was a misconception of who he was
because the brawl at, against Detroit

00:19:24.997 --> 00:19:27.457
in the mid-2000s.

00:19:27.457 --> 00:19:30.127
And I think he was labeled such
as, you know, as I was labeled

00:19:30.127 --> 00:19:32.004
throughout my career, as a thug,
as a problem-maker,

00:19:32.588 --> 00:19:34.923
you know, someone you
can't work with. But, you know,

00:19:34.923 --> 00:19:37.509
our teammates would tell the world
everything but that. You know, we

00:19:37.509 --> 00:19:38.927
were always labeled as great teammates.

00:19:38.927 --> 00:19:39.845
They would always have our backs.

00:19:39.845 --> 00:19:44.892
So to see his transition, one, shedding
his old image and reputation, but

00:19:44.892 --> 00:19:48.854
then two, stepping into quote, unquote
the fire with this

00:19:49.646 --> 00:19:54.526
George Floyd situation. Like
he said, it wasn't something he planned,

00:19:54.526 --> 00:19:57.237
it wasn't something he wanted, but it
was something that was put on his plate.

00:19:57.237 --> 00:19:59.364
And I think he's done a tremendous job of

00:20:00.574 --> 00:20:01.950
handling what's come with that.

00:20:01.992 --> 00:20:05.370
You know, I've been in this social justice
space for maybe about four or five years.

00:20:05.370 --> 00:20:08.498
I did the Stephon Clark stuff up
north in Sacramento, my hometown.

00:20:09.166 --> 00:20:11.251
And even though I'm doing stuff right

00:20:11.293 --> 00:20:15.255
and speaking on the behalf of the people
who don't have a voice, you're still

00:20:15.255 --> 00:20:17.132
gonna take a lot of criticism from people.

00:20:17.424 --> 00:20:21.470
And, you know, that's something
that Jack is getting now, you

00:20:21.470 --> 00:20:23.055
know? So we're talking here and there.

00:20:23.639 --> 00:20:26.475
I know his plate is full, but, you know, I
just hit him every once in a while just to

00:20:26.475 --> 00:20:30.229
remind the man of how proud I am of
him, how much I love him and to really

00:20:30.229 --> 00:20:31.730
take care of himself, you know what I mean?

00:20:31.730 --> 00:20:34.608
Cuz this, just all this negativity,
and I'm sure you’ve felt it too,

00:20:34.608 --> 00:20:37.486
it just, it's disheartening, and
it sucks the life out of you.

00:20:37.486 --> 00:20:37.986
You know what I mean?

00:20:37.986 --> 00:20:40.739
And him to be on the front
line still in Minnesota

00:20:41.114 --> 00:20:42.491
nearly two weeks later,

00:20:42.950 --> 00:20:46.453
I just tell him, you know, to protect
his essence, protect his

00:20:46.453 --> 00:20:49.623
mental health, make sure he gets rest,
make sure he's taking time for him

00:20:49.623 --> 00:20:53.710
because, you know, all this negativity is
not good for anybody, you know? But he's

00:20:53.710 --> 00:20:57.047
doing it for a great reason, but there's
still a lot of pushback, and you see a

00:20:57.047 --> 00:21:00.509
lot of people talking negative about him
and his family now and calling him an

00:21:00.509 --> 00:21:01.969
opportunist and all this kind of stuff

00:21:02.427 --> 00:21:04.513
when at the end of the day,
he's doing what's right,

00:21:05.055 --> 00:21:06.014
and he's a part,

00:21:06.014 --> 00:21:09.268
and he's—when we look back on this
in 20 or 30 years, Jack is gonna be

00:21:09.268 --> 00:21:14.022
one of the main people that people think
about as far as turning this movement.

00:21:14.231 --> 00:21:17.484
You know, there's—obviously George
Floyd wasn't the first, and he won't

00:21:17.484 --> 00:21:22.447
be the last, but I think for the first
time in 400 years, the world hears us.

00:21:22.698 --> 00:21:26.034
And it's because this man was executed
in front of us and the world saw it.

00:21:26.785 --> 00:21:29.413
- Yeah.
- So that was the tipping point finally.

00:21:29.454 --> 00:21:31.790
And although I never, and you
probably feel the same way,

00:21:31.790 --> 00:21:36.586
I didn't agree with the looting and
stealing stuff and burning stuff, but

00:21:36.586 --> 00:21:40.090
at the same time, they've always had a
problem with every way we've protested.

00:21:40.382 --> 00:21:40.757
You know what I mean?
- Yeah.

00:21:41.508 --> 00:21:43.510
You gotta do what you gotta do at some point.

00:21:43.510 --> 00:21:46.513
- We’ve protested silently. We’ve done marches.
Kap took a knee and was blackballed.

00:21:46.513 --> 00:21:50.100
So, you know, enough was enough,
and there was some casualties of wars,

00:21:50.100 --> 00:21:51.685
and that was small businesses and stuff.

00:21:51.727 --> 00:21:55.772
But for the first time, they hear
us now, and I think Jack's platform

00:21:55.772 --> 00:21:57.065
brought a lot of light to that.

00:21:57.065 --> 00:21:59.276
And obviously there were the
Shaun Kings and other athletes

00:21:59.276 --> 00:22:00.360
and other actors, but I think

00:22:00.485 --> 00:22:04.531
Stephen's connection to George Floyd
made it that much more

00:22:04.531 --> 00:22:08.118
special that he was a part of it and someone
that’s leading the movement forward.

00:22:08.327 --> 00:22:12.664
- We talk about the movement, and
the movement is now touching

00:22:12.664 --> 00:22:14.291
a lot of different areas.

00:22:14.583 --> 00:22:18.462
I had just reported that I had
been talking to multiple NBA

00:22:18.462 --> 00:22:19.796
players, and they said that

00:22:20.213 --> 00:22:22.674
a couple days ago, there was
a call with about 50 of them

00:22:22.716 --> 00:22:24.009
talking about what they could do.

00:22:24.343 --> 00:22:27.512
And tonight, they plan on having
a call with a much larger number,

00:22:27.554 --> 00:22:29.431
figuring out what they wanna do.

00:22:29.806 --> 00:22:34.102
Have you been privy to those
calls, and what is kind of the chatter?

00:22:34.102 --> 00:22:35.979
I know you talked a little
bit about it.

00:22:36.104 --> 00:22:39.024
- I don't really know what's going
on in those conversations.

00:22:39.024 --> 00:22:42.152
I've had conversations with a
couple guys that were kinda like,

00:22:43.153 --> 00:22:47.407
“You know, do we play in the midst of
all this, in the midst of the pandemic?”

00:22:47.699 --> 00:22:53.038
You know, but I really feel like
right now, more than ever, the Black

00:22:53.246 --> 00:22:56.750
voice is heard, but the Black athlete
voice has never been stronger and

00:22:56.750 --> 00:22:58.502
may never be stronger, you know?

00:22:58.502 --> 00:23:00.712
So whatever they do, and
the athlete in me obviously

00:23:00.712 --> 00:23:01.797
doesn't want to see them sit out.

00:23:02.255 --> 00:23:04.925
I miss basketball as much as everyone
else does, as much as they do,

00:23:05.300 --> 00:23:09.054
and I wanna see it get back to the
court. But at the same time, being able

00:23:09.054 --> 00:23:13.892
to watch this Blackballed documentary of
my Clipper team from 2014, being

00:23:13.892 --> 00:23:17.020
in the moment, obviously, as players and
feeling like we had a chance to win a

00:23:17.020 --> 00:23:20.941
championship, we wanted to play because
we didn't know what the outcome of

00:23:20.941 --> 00:23:23.860
sitting out was, but we also didn't know
the magnitude and the message it would

00:23:23.860 --> 00:23:25.487
have sent if we did sit out, you know?

00:23:25.487 --> 00:23:28.615
So I hope that everyone got a chance
to see that and kinda learn because

00:23:28.615 --> 00:23:32.661
me looking at it six years ago,
removed, being six years removed,

00:23:33.161 --> 00:23:34.538
I would’ve urged my team to sit out.

00:23:34.704 --> 00:23:36.957
You know, I was the one that
came up with the idea of what

00:23:36.957 --> 00:23:40.127
to do as far as the jerseys and
flip and all this kinda stuff.

00:23:40.127 --> 00:23:43.255
And we did have conversations about not
playing, and Golden State was on board

00:23:43.255 --> 00:23:47.300
with us as well, but we did—there
was too much unknown. But I think now

00:23:47.300 --> 00:23:51.388
we're at a point where, like I said,
our voice has never been stronger.

00:23:51.638 --> 00:23:53.223
Although I don't want them to sit,

00:23:53.223 --> 00:23:58.603
I would understand if they sat out
a game or did something to really

00:23:59.312 --> 00:24:01.148
let everyone know that this is not OK,

00:24:01.148 --> 00:24:03.692
we're unified, and we need to be heard.

00:24:03.900 --> 00:24:06.862
So, to get back to your point, I
haven't been on those conversations,

00:24:06.945 --> 00:24:11.032
and I don't really pry. I'm
someone—I'm a quote, unquote OG now.

00:24:11.032 --> 00:24:13.452
So people just randomly
reach out to me, kinda ask me

00:24:13.869 --> 00:24:15.036
what I think and my thoughts.

00:24:15.036 --> 00:24:17.789
So I have spoke to a few
players on that

00:24:18.290 --> 00:24:20.584
front, and I don't preach. I
don't tell ‘em to sit out.

00:24:20.667 --> 00:24:23.253
I just kind of give ‘em the whole
magnitude of the situation and

00:24:23.253 --> 00:24:24.713
understanding “Whatever you guys do,

00:24:25.088 --> 00:24:26.089
it's gonna be powerful.

00:24:26.089 --> 00:24:28.425
So just make sure you're
united in that movement.”

00:24:29.676 --> 00:24:29.759
- Mhmm.

00:24:29.843 --> 00:24:38.602
What would you say to Adam Silver if
he asked you the best way to address

00:24:38.727 --> 00:24:39.311
racism in the league?

00:24:40.604 --> 00:24:41.938
- Ooh, that’s a good question.

00:24:41.938 --> 00:24:45.650
I think all the teams should start a fund,

00:24:46.401 --> 00:24:51.031
you know, and really, and
individual funds for their cities

00:24:51.448 --> 00:24:56.036
and look to help their
communities, not just put out PSAs or

00:24:56.453 --> 00:25:02.709
cheap PR things of guys saying stuff,
because, you know, how most of this stuff

00:25:02.709 --> 00:25:05.795
goes is it’s talked about and then
a couple weeks later, we get back to

00:25:05.795 --> 00:25:07.088
real life and kind of forget about it.

00:25:07.380 --> 00:25:10.717
I would love to see each team
start some sort of fund that is

00:25:10.717 --> 00:25:12.385
community-based in their city,

00:25:12.636 --> 00:25:14.554
and it can go to a
number of different things.

00:25:14.554 --> 00:25:18.808
Whatever that team feels is
lacking in their minority, in the

00:25:18.808 --> 00:25:20.810
minority culture of their cities.

00:25:21.520 --> 00:25:28.485
You know, I had a phone call with
Rob Pelinka maybe two weeks ago

00:25:29.236 --> 00:25:33.198
out of the blue. And he kind of just hit
me up like, “Hey, Matt, you know, we're

00:25:33.198 --> 00:25:38.620
looking for things to do as, as the
Lakers, you know, as a, as

00:25:38.620 --> 00:25:43.166
an organization to really unite and be
a part of this movement and

00:25:43.166 --> 00:25:44.834
be an effective part of this movement.”

00:25:44.918 --> 00:25:46.253
And it kinda took me off guard.

00:25:46.253 --> 00:25:48.463
I'm like, “The Lakers
are calling me to ask?”

00:25:49.422 --> 00:25:52.217
I mean, so, I told them—I was
like, “Man, that's a heavy question.

00:25:52.509 --> 00:25:53.218
I got Zooms.

00:25:53.218 --> 00:25:53.885
I got my kids.

00:25:53.885 --> 00:25:55.762
Give me till the end of the day
and I'll send you some ideas.”

00:25:55.762 --> 00:25:59.266
So I sent him some ideas, but the first
thing—I said it earlier—it’s just like,

00:25:59.266 --> 00:26:01.268
“This can't be just a PR move to save face.”

00:26:01.518 --> 00:26:01.935
You know what I mean?

00:26:01.935 --> 00:26:04.229
This can't just be, “Let me,
let me put a Lakers statement out,

00:26:04.229 --> 00:26:07.065
let the Lakers know you're
with us.” To me, either

00:26:07.232 --> 00:26:08.149
don't say nothing—

00:26:08.441 --> 00:26:10.068
if you get in, you gotta really get in.

00:26:10.277 --> 00:26:11.444
You gotta be entrenched.

00:26:11.444 --> 00:26:12.779
There's things you can do.

00:26:13.196 --> 00:26:15.532
And I gave him a list of things they
can do, and I'm gonna probably

00:26:15.532 --> 00:26:18.868
talk to him this week to kind of
touch base and see what they decided.

00:26:18.868 --> 00:26:22.747
But you know, to me, you gotta be all
the way in, and it’s seen that the NBA

00:26:22.747 --> 00:26:24.916
is so progressive, and we usually are

00:26:25.709 --> 00:26:28.628
the ones that are first to
do stuff and people follow.

00:26:29.045 --> 00:26:33.091
I would just love to see each team
start a fund because, you know, these

00:26:33.091 --> 00:26:35.844
organizations are making so much
money off these players, and although

00:26:35.844 --> 00:26:37.554
the players have tremendous contracts,

00:26:37.762 --> 00:26:39.931
you gotta think, if our
contracts are so well,

00:26:39.931 --> 00:26:41.016
how much are the owners making?

00:26:41.141 --> 00:26:43.810
You know, if they're able to pay
Steph and LeBron and these guys

00:26:43.810 --> 00:26:45.812
$200 million a year—excuse me,

00:26:46.146 --> 00:26:47.856
$200 million contracts—
imagine how much

00:26:47.856 --> 00:26:49.566
money these teams are making.

00:26:49.566 --> 00:26:53.028
So you start these funds and
consistently doing stuff.

00:26:53.069 --> 00:26:55.905
Don't give me a one-time
donation. Make it, make it a—

00:26:55.905 --> 00:26:58.575
- Make this a cornerstone of your franchise.
- Right.

00:26:59.242 --> 00:27:02.454
- Make it a yearly thing, make it a once-a-year
huge donation, but let's

00:27:02.495 --> 00:27:03.705
build something from here.

00:27:03.705 --> 00:27:04.914
And that's what I was
trying to tell the Lakers is,

00:27:05.165 --> 00:27:08.293
“If you guys do something
trendsetting or groundbreaking,

00:27:08.293 --> 00:27:09.127
you guys are the Lakers.

00:27:09.127 --> 00:27:11.921
So I think other teams are gonna fall
in line, not only in basketball, but

00:27:11.921 --> 00:27:13.381
professional sports across the board.”

00:27:13.381 --> 00:27:13.632
So,

00:27:14.507 --> 00:27:15.759
it’s gonna be interesting.

00:27:15.800 --> 00:27:20.055
I've always been a fan of Adam
Silver and his outside-the-box thinking.

00:27:20.764 --> 00:27:23.224
But I think the NBA is so progressive,

00:27:23.224 --> 00:27:24.934
it has to be something that's heartfelt,

00:27:24.934 --> 00:27:25.644
that's meaningful,

00:27:25.644 --> 00:27:26.770
that won't just go away,

00:27:26.978 --> 00:27:28.855
you know, once we're done talking about
George Floyd.

00:27:29.939 --> 00:27:30.732
- And you're right. The

00:27:30.732 --> 00:27:34.736
NBA is so progressive, probably
the most progressive of the leagues.

00:27:34.736 --> 00:27:37.322
I think Adam Silver is a
fantastic leader, and the things

00:27:37.322 --> 00:27:39.324
that he has done for the league.

00:27:39.949 --> 00:27:43.995
You, however, were in the thick
of a scandal with the league

00:27:43.995 --> 00:27:47.624
when everybody found out that,
you know, Donald Sterling was racist.

00:27:47.957 --> 00:27:48.124
- Right.

00:27:48.708 --> 00:27:56.841
- But I guess, and I mean symbolically,
how many, if any, Donald Sterlings

00:27:57.092 --> 00:28:01.096
still exist in NBA front offices?

00:28:01.096 --> 00:28:02.097
- A lot.

00:28:02.722 --> 00:28:03.264
A lot.

00:28:03.264 --> 00:28:05.266
And not only in the NBA; in the NFL,

00:28:05.266 --> 00:28:06.017
I know for sure.

00:28:06.476 --> 00:28:07.185
You know what I mean?

00:28:07.185 --> 00:28:11.314
So it's just, and I don't—you don't
wanna give names, but there's

00:28:12.190 --> 00:28:17.237
that good ol’ boy group of owners that
were oil or real estate guys. These

00:28:17.237 --> 00:28:21.616
guys are in there, you know, 70s,
late 60s, 70s and up.

00:28:21.825 --> 00:28:24.869
And that's just how they were
raised and how they were brought up.

00:28:25.286 --> 00:28:25.787
You know what I mean?

00:28:25.787 --> 00:28:30.166
And they look at players as—I didn't
wanna use the word “slave,” that's

00:28:30.166 --> 00:28:31.668
harsh—but just these are my, like—

00:28:32.711 --> 00:28:35.130
a kind of a plantation view or mentality.

00:28:35.130 --> 00:28:36.172
Like, “These are my guys.

00:28:36.172 --> 00:28:36.881
I pay them.”

00:28:36.881 --> 00:28:40.301
Like Donald Sterling
said, “I get them cars.

00:28:40.301 --> 00:28:41.094
I get them food.

00:28:41.094 --> 00:28:44.723
I get them houses.” But he wasn't
the only one to think like that.

00:28:44.723 --> 00:28:47.767
He's just the only one dumb enough
to get caught, but I guarantee

00:28:47.767 --> 00:28:49.519
there's owners in these leagues

00:28:50.061 --> 00:28:53.314
and, in particular, if you look at it, how
many owners have really came out and spoke

00:28:53.481 --> 00:28:59.446
on behalf of us? And not a generic—like
Roger Goodell read off the thing and still

00:28:59.446 --> 00:29:01.531
didn’t even address the Kap situation.

00:29:02.157 --> 00:29:04.909
But how many owners have really
come out and spoke on our behalf?

00:29:05.243 --> 00:29:07.996
How many of these college coaches have
come out and spoke on our behalf that

00:29:07.996 --> 00:29:11.624
ride these Black players in football and
basketball to where they're the highest-paid

00:29:11.624 --> 00:29:16.045
college coaches around the country and their
programs are benefiting? Not too many.

00:29:16.838 --> 00:29:18.798
And to me, their silence speaks volumes.

00:29:19.340 --> 00:29:19.716
You know what I mean?

00:29:19.716 --> 00:29:23.094
You wanna go into our house
as college players and “I'm

00:29:23.094 --> 00:29:24.971
gonna take care of your son
I'm gonna do whatever.

00:29:24.971 --> 00:29:26.681
He's gonna love it in Lexington.”

00:29:26.681 --> 00:29:30.727
And “He's gonna love it in Oklahoma,”
or “He's gonna love it at LSU.”

00:29:31.311 --> 00:29:34.647
But now when when the, you know,
the shit quote, unquote really hits

00:29:34.647 --> 00:29:37.192
the fan, you're getting these generic,

00:29:38.777 --> 00:29:42.489
you know, statements that PR people wrote
for these coaches to read, and I just wish

00:29:42.489 --> 00:29:45.742
it would be more heartfelt and you had
that same energy when you're trying to

00:29:45.742 --> 00:29:47.452
recruit us to win national championships

00:29:47.494 --> 00:29:50.538
now that you have for us
when our backs are against the

00:29:50.538 --> 00:29:51.706
wall and we're in this fire.

00:29:52.165 --> 00:29:52.373
- Yeah.

00:29:52.874 --> 00:29:55.043
And—I'm not asking you to name
names—when I asked you the

00:29:55.043 --> 00:29:56.711
question, you said, “For sure.”

00:29:57.086 --> 00:30:02.050
Are there, like, instances in your head
that make you sure, you know, that there

00:30:02.050 --> 00:30:03.718
are these people that still exist?

00:30:04.177 --> 00:30:04.677
- Yes.

00:30:04.928 --> 00:30:08.306
I've seen situations that have
happened personally to me.

00:30:08.389 --> 00:30:10.058
I've been around other owners.

00:30:10.099 --> 00:30:11.518
I've played for several teams.

00:30:11.518 --> 00:30:12.519
I've talked to different

00:30:13.102 --> 00:30:17.524
players about situations,
and not only in basketball,

00:30:17.524 --> 00:30:22.403
in football, and it's just kind
of an unspoken thing, you know,

00:30:22.403 --> 00:30:24.072
but it's definitely still there.

00:30:24.197 --> 00:30:25.532
It's definitely prevalent.

00:30:26.115 --> 00:30:30.119
And, you know, I think the fact that
they had to make the Rooney Rule in the

00:30:30.119 --> 00:30:34.833
NFL, which is crazy to think that you
had to make a rule that teams would

00:30:35.375 --> 00:30:39.879
at least interview one minority person
for management jobs tells you their

00:30:39.879 --> 00:30:42.549
train of thought and the lines
of their thinking, because most of

00:30:42.549 --> 00:30:44.759
the time they're just looking over
us or not even giving us

00:30:44.759 --> 00:30:46.427
an opportunity, you know, for those.

00:30:46.427 --> 00:30:50.181
So some people say, you know,
“Those jobs aren't racially filled.

00:30:50.181 --> 00:30:51.891
It's just the best man gets the job.”

00:30:52.559 --> 00:30:55.061
And for the most part, that's true,
but the best Black candidates

00:30:55.061 --> 00:30:56.020
aren't even getting a chance.

00:30:56.396 --> 00:30:56.938
- Yeah.

00:30:57.438 --> 00:31:00.316
- The best minority candidates aren’t even getting a
chance to interview, you know? So I'm sure you

00:31:00.316 --> 00:31:02.235
probably hired the best white person

00:31:02.235 --> 00:31:06.531
you felt fit the job, but it's because
you didn't give any Black people a

00:31:06.531 --> 00:31:08.241
chance to even interview for the job.

00:31:08.658 --> 00:31:11.160
- I was watching an interview
that you did with Vlad,

00:31:11.786 --> 00:31:15.999
and you had this quote. You said,
"To me, there will always be racism.

00:31:16.082 --> 00:31:17.876
Racism is something that's taught.

00:31:18.042 --> 00:31:20.003
We have to learn to maneuver within that."

00:31:20.712 --> 00:31:26.676
So my question, is that what you still
believe, and if so, how do we win

00:31:27.176 --> 00:31:30.430
If racism will always exist?

00:31:31.014 --> 00:31:32.640
- I think it's always gonna exist,

00:31:32.640 --> 00:31:34.100
and I still do agree with that.

00:31:34.100 --> 00:31:36.436
But I think we're to a point now where

00:31:37.896 --> 00:31:41.649
we need more white allies, and
we're starting to gain those.

00:31:41.649 --> 00:31:45.278
And I think the one thing we saw
through these protests and rallies and

00:31:45.278 --> 00:31:48.990
riots, whatever you wanna call it,
we had every race out there.

00:31:49.407 --> 00:31:52.744
You know, every skin color,
every religion, everyone came

00:31:52.744 --> 00:31:54.037
together, you know what I mean?

00:31:54.037 --> 00:31:58.750
So I think we need more people that
are for us and with us

00:31:58.791 --> 00:32:00.668
and have an open mind to the task.

00:32:00.752 --> 00:32:03.922
Cuz we used to give people a pass
saying, “Well, you know, they didn't grow

00:32:03.922 --> 00:32:06.883
up that way, or “They're white,” or
and “That's why they don't understand this.”

00:32:06.883 --> 00:32:09.093
And that used to be an
OK pass that people get.

00:32:09.093 --> 00:32:10.136
But now it's not OK.

00:32:10.261 --> 00:32:13.056
You know, now you have to have
an understanding, and I think

00:32:13.056 --> 00:32:14.098
people are starting to do that.

00:32:14.724 --> 00:32:15.141
So,

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:18.728
I just feel like we need
to call people on racism.

00:32:18.895 --> 00:32:20.772
People need to be fired from their jobs.

00:32:20.772 --> 00:32:22.440
People need to be publicly embarrassed.

00:32:22.440 --> 00:32:26.486
The world needs to know that, you know,
your FedEx driver that is delivering

00:32:26.486 --> 00:32:30.907
your packages, you know, did a protest
the other day with a man, a knee on a

00:32:30.907 --> 00:32:34.911
man's neck talking about, you know, “Eff
George Floyd. Black lives don't matter.

00:32:34.911 --> 00:32:35.912
Only white lives matter.”

00:32:36.371 --> 00:32:36.621
You know?

00:32:36.621 --> 00:32:41.084
So I think more than ever, you know,
the Black culture quote, unquote wants

00:32:41.084 --> 00:32:43.169
to be on this cancel culture.

00:32:44.045 --> 00:32:44.128
But to be

00:32:44.295 --> 00:32:47.006
honest with you, this cancel culture
now more than ever is gonna be

00:32:47.006 --> 00:32:50.677
real because we're getting people
on camera who are just blatantly

00:32:50.677 --> 00:32:52.720
showing who they are and their racism.

00:32:53.221 --> 00:32:56.015
And before you know it,
their face is circling and their name’s

00:32:56.015 --> 00:32:57.308
circling, and then they're fired.

00:32:57.767 --> 00:32:58.059
You know?

00:32:58.059 --> 00:33:01.479
So when I say I think we're
gonna have to work within that,

00:33:01.479 --> 00:33:03.856
I think we just need to
continue to have that,

00:33:03.898 --> 00:33:05.316
you know, we always got criticized.

00:33:05.441 --> 00:33:08.653
“Oh, you always wanna bring race in it. Why do
you always wanna pull the race card?” Cuz race

00:33:08.653 --> 00:33:10.530
plays a big part in our everyday lives.

00:33:10.530 --> 00:33:11.531
- In everything.

00:33:11.781 --> 00:33:13.408
- And people finally see that now.

00:33:13.408 --> 00:33:16.035
So, like I said, it's a taught thing.

00:33:16.119 --> 00:33:18.705
Hopefully it's gonna open
up enough eyes for parents to

00:33:18.913 --> 00:33:23.376
start teaching love because your kid is
gonna, you know—like, I had a conversation

00:33:23.376 --> 00:33:27.922
with my twins about the George Floyd
situation, and I asked them, “Is hate taught,

00:33:28.548 --> 00:33:29.465
or are you born with it?”

00:33:29.841 --> 00:33:30.925
And they both hesitated,

00:33:30.925 --> 00:33:34.262
and they're like, “Taught.” I'm like,
“You're absolutely right.

00:33:34.262 --> 00:33:35.638
Like, just like you can teach love.”

00:33:35.972 --> 00:33:37.890
And then they have a—I
have an 18-month-old too.

00:33:37.890 --> 00:33:40.852
So then one of my sons is like, “We're
gonna have to teach Ashton how to love,”

00:33:41.602 --> 00:33:44.230
you know? And it's really that
simple, you know what I mean?

00:33:44.272 --> 00:33:46.899
These kids are sponges, so
they're gonna, they're gonna

00:33:46.899 --> 00:33:50.737
learn and be whatever you feed them, you
know? So if you're teaching them love,

00:33:51.404 --> 00:33:52.447
they're gonna have a better chance.

00:33:52.447 --> 00:33:55.366
So, like I said, I still think
there's too many people in this

00:33:55.366 --> 00:33:56.367
world that hate for no reason.

00:33:56.367 --> 00:34:00.496
If you asked to a KKK member
or a white person, like, “Why

00:34:00.496 --> 00:34:01.497
don't you like Black people?”

00:34:01.539 --> 00:34:02.665
They couldn't give you no real answer.
- Mhmm.

00:34:03.374 --> 00:34:06.044
- They're gonna give you
a stereotypical answer.

00:34:06.252 --> 00:34:09.380
“One of them robbed my cousin,” or “They
do this,” or “They're on welfare.”

00:34:09.380 --> 00:34:10.923
They can't give you no real answers.

00:34:10.923 --> 00:34:11.966
So they're just hating

00:34:12.425 --> 00:34:12.925
to hate.

00:34:13.092 --> 00:34:17.430
So I hope this movement changes some
of that, but I'm not naive enough to

00:34:17.430 --> 00:34:18.723
think it's just gonna disappear.

00:34:18.723 --> 00:34:22.268
So I think we're just gonna have
to learn to maneuver within it, but

00:34:22.769 --> 00:34:25.938
more white people are gonna have
to start calling people on this stuff.

00:34:25.938 --> 00:34:28.900
Just like bad cops are gonna start
having to call bad cops on their stuff.

00:34:28.900 --> 00:34:33.863
I just think the more we expose
this hate, it’ll eventually,

00:34:34.530 --> 00:34:36.657
you know, not go away,
but hopefully shrink.

00:34:38.201 --> 00:34:41.662
- I'm ending every interview in
this series with this question,

00:34:42.538 --> 00:34:46.167
and that's that words mean different
things to different people.

00:34:46.584 --> 00:34:49.545
Matt, how do you define
America?

00:34:49.962 --> 00:34:51.172
- A gift and a curse.

00:34:52.006 --> 00:34:59.472
I think that we're very lucky in
some aspects to be here and, and

00:35:00.640 --> 00:35:04.102
live this quote, unquote dream, because
although it's been tough for me,

00:35:04.185 --> 00:35:05.269
I've been able to live a dream.

00:35:05.269 --> 00:35:08.898
I’ve faced a ton of racism in my 40
years, but if I sit back and look at

00:35:08.898 --> 00:35:13.027
the big picture, I played 14 years
in the NBA, made a lot of money,

00:35:13.319 --> 00:35:16.781
have a beautiful family, and I'm
thriving in business and doing

00:35:16.781 --> 00:35:18.157
things to try to help other people.

00:35:18.157 --> 00:35:20.993
But at the same time, I was the lucky one,

00:35:21.285 --> 00:35:24.247
you know? We both beat the odds to
be able to do what you're doing,

00:35:24.247 --> 00:35:25.248
to be able to do what I'm doing.

00:35:25.248 --> 00:35:25.998
We beat the odds.

00:35:25.998 --> 00:35:28.793
So we realized that some of
our people aren't as lucky.

00:35:29.335 --> 00:35:32.922
And that's where I say it's a curse
because I've had personal friends,

00:35:32.922 --> 00:35:36.425
and we've seen people that we don't
even know but have grown fond of, lose their

00:35:36.425 --> 00:35:38.052
life because of the color of their skin.

00:35:38.052 --> 00:35:43.474
So it definitely means, the flag means
something different to most Black people.

00:35:43.766 --> 00:35:46.144
America means something
different to most Black people.

00:35:47.478 --> 00:35:51.440
But I think at the end of the
day, we are lucky to an extent.

00:35:51.774 --> 00:35:56.445
And I think now more than ever, our voices
are gonna be heard, and we have a chance

00:35:56.445 --> 00:36:01.909
to change what America means and what
that flag means for our children, our

00:36:02.201 --> 00:36:04.662
grandchildren and generations to come.

00:36:06.038 --> 00:36:06.998
- That was beautiful.

00:36:06.998 --> 00:36:08.082
Thank you so much, Matt.

00:36:08.082 --> 00:36:08.541
- No problem.

00:36:08.583 --> 00:36:09.083
Thanks for having me.

00:36:09.083 --> 00:36:09.417
Good luck.

